1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,429
Coach: When a discussion slows down, the next useful move is not always to speak more.

2
00:00:06,429 --> 00:00:11,143
Coach: First notice what is happening and what the group still needs.

3
00:00:11,143 --> 00:00:13,714
Coach: A short pause, an unfinished explanation

4
00:00:13,714 --> 00:00:17,143
Coach: and two overlapping turns call for different judgements.

5
00:00:17,493 --> 00:00:19,207
Coach: Use this practice task.

6
00:00:19,207 --> 00:00:23,921
Coach: The activity’s twenty-minute limit is a condition of the plan,

7
00:00:23,921 --> 00:00:26,493
Coach: not a timer for the discussion.

8
00:00:26,493 --> 00:00:31,207
Coach: No recommendation has been adopted and no activity has taken place.

9
00:00:31,557 --> 00:00:36,700
undefined: A community centre wants a 20-minute welcome activity for new members.

10
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:42,271
undefined: Help them meet one another and find out about its weekly drawing group.

11
00:00:42,271 --> 00:00:47,843
undefined: Your group should recommend one activity and explain how it serves both aims.

12
00:00:47,843 --> 00:00:53,414
undefined: Use the main room and existing paper and pens; do not buy anything.

13
00:00:53,764 --> 00:00:58,050
Maya: We could start in pairs and exchange names and interests.

14
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:00,543
Coach: Maya has offered paired introductions.

15
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:05,686
Coach: Exchanging names and interests could provide a way for people to meet.

16
00:01:05,686 --> 00:01:12,543
Coach: Her sentence has not yet explained how newcomers would find out about the weekly drawing group.

17
00:01:12,543 --> 00:01:14,257
Coach: That link remains open.

18
00:01:14,607 --> 00:01:17,607
Coach: An open link is something to develop,

19
00:01:17,607 --> 00:01:22,750
Coach: not proof that the idea is poor or its speaker is unprepared.

20
00:01:22,750 --> 00:01:28,750
Coach: A useful next contribution might explore that connection instead of replacing the whole proposal.

21
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:32,100
Coach: Privately separate a fact from an interpretation.

22
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:34,243
Coach: What has Maya actually suggested,

23
00:01:34,243 --> 00:01:38,957
Coach: and what question does the task still leave us to examine?

24
00:01:38,957 --> 00:01:42,386
Coach: Stay with the words and the shared aim.

25
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:55,450
Coach: A pause does not tell you whether others agree, are thinking,

26
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:58,879
Coach: have missed a point or prefer another idea.

27
00:01:58,879 --> 00:02:00,164
Coach: Those are possibilities.

28
00:02:00,164 --> 00:02:02,307
Coach: Unless someone provides more information,

29
00:02:02,307 --> 00:02:05,307
Coach: the cause of that pause remains unknown.

30
00:02:05,657 --> 00:02:10,800
Coach: Likewise, beginning at the same moment does not by itself establish hostility.

31
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:18,514
Coach: One person speaking less in a short excerpt does not make them the quiet member of the group.

32
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:21,943
Coach: Describe the moment rather than assigning a personality.

33
00:02:22,293 --> 00:02:25,293
Coach: Sometimes leaving a little space is useful.

34
00:02:25,293 --> 00:02:27,436
Coach: Sometimes a focused question helps.

35
00:02:27,436 --> 00:02:32,579
Coach: There is no single pause length after which someone must take charge,

36
00:02:32,579 --> 00:02:38,150
Coach: and no need to appoint yourself the leader to make a small contribution.

37
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:41,929
Coach: Ask what your next words would make easier:

38
00:02:41,929 --> 00:02:44,500
Coach: understanding an idea, finishing a turn,

39
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:48,786
Coach: connecting a proposal to the task, or hearing another view.

40
00:02:48,786 --> 00:02:53,929
Coach: Choose the immediate need rather than trying to manage everything at once.

41
00:02:54,279 --> 00:03:01,993
Coach: For this proposal, asking how it could introduce the drawing group would bring attention to a missing link.

42
00:03:01,993 --> 00:03:05,422
Coach: The question would not supply the programme details

43
00:03:05,422 --> 00:03:07,993
Coach: or prove that the activity works.

44
00:03:07,993 --> 00:03:10,993
Coach: Those still need to be worked out.

45
00:03:11,343 --> 00:03:14,772
Coach: Privately choose one small move for the excerpt.

46
00:03:14,772 --> 00:03:18,629
Coach: You may leave space or form a focused question.

47
00:03:18,629 --> 00:03:22,914
Coach: Think about what it would help the group do next,

48
00:03:22,914 --> 00:03:26,343
Coach: rather than how it would make you appear.

49
00:03:34,693 --> 00:03:39,407
Coach: After a small move, leave room and attend to the reply.

50
00:03:39,407 --> 00:03:44,979
Coach: Its usefulness depends on what happens next, not just on a polite phrase.

51
00:03:44,979 --> 00:03:50,550
Coach: We will start with one fresh example of a pause after a proposal.
